* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* GRANT <account> TO <user>
* REVOKE <account> FROM <user>
* LIST.GRANTS <account>
*
* THE GRANT IS LINUX GROUP MEMBERSHIP, NOT A FIELD IN THE RECORD.  The port's
* model and the owner's decision of 14 Aug 2026.  Entry to an account is
* membership of the group named in its ACC$GROUP, which CREATE.ACCOUNT writes
* as sdu_<name>, and which LOGIN:323 and CPROC's int.logto both test.  So these
* verbs edit that group and write NOTHING to the ACCOUNTS record.
*
* THREE VERBS, ONE PROGRAM, distinguished by the verb token.  "GRANT <account>"
* with no TO clause lists, because it is the natural thing to try and answering
* it costs nothing.
*
* WHY LISTING NEEDED BUILDING AT ALL.  The grant is not a field, so "who may
* enter this account" is not a question the dictionary can answer, and an
* account whose grants cannot be listed cannot be audited by eye.  !grp_members
* is this tree's answer to the port's !os_group LISTMEM.
*
* ***$GRANTA IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN CPROC's privileged_commands, WHICH IS THE
* ONE PLACE THIS DIVERGES FROM ITS NEIGHBOURS.***  $CREATEA, $DELACC and
* $MODIFYA are on that list (CPROC:197-201) and run with euid 0 restored around
* the call.  This verb needs none of it, measured 11 Sep 2026:
*
*   - it only READS the register.  /usr/local/sdsys/ACCOUNTS records are
*     -rw-r--r-- root:root, so any session can read them; MODIFY.ACCOUNT is on
*     the list because it WRITES the register and another account's VOC.
*   - the audit trail is sdsys:sdusers 0620, so an sdusers member appends to it
*     without privilege - which is the whole design of PORT_ADOPTION 13.
*   - the group edits go through "sudo sd-elevate", and sudo decides on the
*     REAL uid, which is the person and not the euid this would have changed.
*     /etc/sudoers.d/sdcore is "%sdadmin ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: sd-elevate", and
*     kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR) below is already true only for somebody in
*     sdadmin (CPROC's grant.administrator).
*   - OS.EXECUTE is permitted because this program is $internal, which is
*     os_permitted()'s FIRST answer (op_sh.c:136-144) and does not depend on
*     the session's privilege at all.
*
* So putting it on the list would run the whole verb as root to gain nothing.
* ***IF A LATER CHANGE MAKES THIS VERB WRITE THE REGISTER, IT GOES ON THE LIST
* AND THIS PARAGRAPH COMES OFF.***
*
* THE ACCOUNT'S OWN USER IS IN THE GROUP, AND SO IS root, and both are listed
* like any other member.  CREATE.ACCOUNT builds the group as
* "groupadd <group> root,sdsys,<user>" (CREATEA:876), so neither is a grant
* somebody made.  Hiding them would make this disagree with the group it is
* reporting, and quietly disagreeing with the operating system is worse than a
* line that needs a moment's thought.  ***MEASURED 11 Sep 2026: the five
* sdu_ groups on this machine are root,<user> - sdsys is NOT in any of them,
* though CREATEA passes it.  Why is an open lead (PROJECT_STATUS START HERE);
* it changes nothing here, because this reports what the group holds.***
*
* THE AUDIT RECORD IS WRITTEN AFTER EACH SUCCESSFUL GROUP EDIT, the port's
* step 5f and its PRE_RELEASE 98 ordering: a trail must not record a right that
* was not given.  The identity is stamped by audit_message() in k_error.c and
* is not passed from here, so a caller cannot get it wrong or forge it.
*
* THERE IS NO EQUIVALENT OF THE PORT'S "rc = 5 MEANS NOT ELEVATED", so
* group.failed does not pretend to one.  !os_group returned a small set of
* documented codes; here OS.ERROR() is the child's exit status
* (op_sh.c:351), and 1 is sudo refusing the command AND usermod failing AND
* gpasswd failing.  Guessing between them would be the false-precision the
* instrument rule warns about.  sd-elevate prints its own refusal to stderr -
* "sd-elevate: REFUSED - ..." - and OS.EXECUTE passes that through to the
* terminal, so the precise reason is already on screen; 10050 adds the status.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $grant.account
$catalog $GRANTA

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h

deffun is_grp_member(user,group) calling '!is_grp_member'
deffun is_user(uname) calling '!is_user'
deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'
deffun grp_members(group) calling '!grp_members'
* 11 Sep 26 dm - the tier ordering, PORT_ADOPTION 14.  The COURTESY caller;
*   CPROC's int.logto is the one that holds.
deffun tier_allows(user,account) calling '!tier_allows'

   prompt ''

   parser = "!PARSER"

* IT SAYS SO BEFORE IT TRIES.  sd-elevate would refuse a caller sudo does not
* permit, but the refusal a person sees should be SD's, in SD's words, and not
* a line of shell diagnostics.  CREATEA:127 gates the same way.
   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS     ;* Preset for argument errors

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, verb, keyword)

   verb = upcase(verb)
   listing = (verb = 'LIST.GRANTS')
   granting = (verb = 'GRANT')

   * Account name, prompted for if it was left off

   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      display sysmsg(6001) :  ;* Account name:
      input acc.name
      acc.name = trim(acc.name)
      if acc.name = '' then stop
   end
   acc.name = upcase(acc.name)

   * TO / FROM clause.  Absent means "list", which is what LIST.GRANTS is.

   user = ''
   if not(listing) then
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, kw, keyword)
      begin case
         case token.type = PARSER$END
            listing = @true
         case granting and upcase(kw) = 'TO'
            gosub get.user
         case (not(granting)) and upcase(kw) = 'FROM'
            gosub get.user
         case 1
            stop sysmsg(2018, kw) ;* Unexpected token (xx)
      end case
   end else
      call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
      if token.type # PARSER$END then
         stop sysmsg(2018, token) ;* Unexpected token (xx)
      end
   end

   gosub resolve.account

   begin case
      case listing
         gosub do.list
      case granting
         gosub do.grant
      case 1
         gosub do.revoke
   end case

   stop

* ======================================================================
* Read the account and find the Linux group that IS the grant.

resolve.account:
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'ACCOUNTS' to acc.f else
      @system.return.code = -status()
      stop sysmsg(6000) ;* Cannot open ACCOUNTS file in SDSYS account
   end

   read acc.rec from acc.f, acc.name else
      @system.return.code = -status()
      stop sysmsg(6027) ;* Account not registered in ACCOUNTS file
   end

*  REFUSED RATHER THAN GUESSED, which is TIERGATE's rule on an unknown tier and
*  the same reasoning.  An empty ACC$GROUP means a record older than
*  CREATE.ACCOUNT's group work.  Inventing "sdu_":acc.name here would create a
*  Linux group nothing else consults and report success, which is a worse
*  outcome than saying the record needs attention.
   grp = trim(acc.rec<ACC$GROUP>)
   if grp = '' then
      @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
      stop sysmsg(10044, acc.name) ;* Account %1 has no Linux group recorded
   end

   return

* ======================================================================

get.user:
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, user, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      stop sysmsg(6074) ;* Invalid user name
   end

   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
   if token.type # PARSER$END then
      stop sysmsg(2018, token) ;* Unexpected token (xx)
   end

   return

* ======================================================================

do.grant:
   if not(is_user(user)) then
      stop sysmsg(10045, user) ;* There is no Linux user named %1
   end

* 11 Sep 26 dm - A GRANT MAY GO DOWN OR SIDEWAYS, NEVER UP.  Owner's rule to
*   the port, 29 Aug 2026: "standard accounts can not be given access to
*   programmer accounts, programmer accounts can be given access to standard
*   accounts.  Only windows administrators can enter SDSYS, rights to SDSYS can
*   not be granted to programmers or standard accounts."
*
*   THIS IS THE COURTESY REFUSAL AND CPROC's IS THE GATE.  Refusing here is
*   what lets the administrator making the grant find out immediately, instead
*   of the user discovering it at LOGTO; but it CANNOT be the whole of the
*   rule, because the grant is Linux group membership and "usermod -aG" makes
*   one without SD's knowledge.  int.logto carries the load.
*
*   BEFORE THE "already a member" TEST, deliberately.  A membership that should
*   not exist - made outside SD, or left behind by a promotion - must not be
*   reported as a cheerful "already", which is the one answer that would stop
*   anybody looking at it.
   if not(tier_allows(user, acc.name)) then
      tg.why = status()
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      begin case
         case tg.why = 4
            stop sysmsg(10126, acc.name, 'SDSYS', user)
         case tg.why = 2
            stop sysmsg(10127, user)  ;* %1 has no SD account, so no tier
         case 1
            stop sysmsg(10126, acc.name, ~
                        upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B')), user)
      end case
   end

*  "usermod -aG" is idempotent and sd-elevate would report success either way.
*  Asking first is what lets this say "already" rather than implying it did
*  something.
   if is_grp_member(user, grp) then
      crt sysmsg(10017, user, grp) ;* %1 is already a member of group %2
      @system.return.code = 0
      return
   end

   if not(valid_os_name(user)) or not(valid_os_name(grp)) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
      stop sysmsg(10050, grp, 2) ;* Cannot change the members of group %1
   end

* 11 Sep 26 dm - NOTE THE ARGUMENT ORDER REVERSES: raw usermod is
*   "-aG <group> <user>", sd-elevate is "addgroup <user> <group>".  The same
*   note stands at MODIFYA:189 and CREATEA:424.
   os.execute "sudo /usr/local/sbin/sd-elevate addgroup ":user:" ":grp
   rc = OS.ERROR()
   if rc # 0 then gosub group.failed

*  After the group edit, so this records what happened and not what was
*  attempted; a failed addgroup leaves through group.failed and never gets here.
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'GRANT account=' : acc.name : ' to=' : user)

   crt sysmsg(10041, user, acc.name) ;* %1 may now use account %2
   gosub say.sign.out

   @system.return.code = 0
   return

* ======================================================================

do.revoke:
*  No is_user test.  Revoking somebody whose Linux user has been deleted is a
*  reasonable thing to want, and is_grp_member answers the question that
*  actually matters here.
   if not(is_grp_member(user, grp)) then
      crt sysmsg(10049, user, acc.name) ;* %1 has not been granted account %2
      @system.return.code = 0
      return
   end

   if not(valid_os_name(user)) or not(valid_os_name(grp)) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
      stop sysmsg(10050, grp, 2) ;* Cannot change the members of group %1
   end

   os.execute "sudo /usr/local/sbin/sd-elevate delgroup ":user:" ":grp
   rc = OS.ERROR()
   if rc # 0 then gosub group.failed

   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'REVOKE account=' : acc.name : ' from=' : user)

   crt sysmsg(10042, user, acc.name) ;* %1 no longer has account %2
   gosub say.sign.out

   @system.return.code = 0
   return

* ======================================================================

do.list:
*  status() IS READ ONCE, INTO A VARIABLE.  It is the last call's status, so
*  asking it twice - once in the test, once in the message - is asking two
*  different questions that happen to agree today.
   members = grp_members(grp)
   gm.rc = status()
   if gm.rc # 0 then
      @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
      stop sysmsg(10048, grp, gm.rc) ;* Cannot read the members of group %1
   end

*  dcount('') IS 1, NOT 0.  Without this an empty group reports one member
*  whose name is the empty string.  !grp_members carries the same two lines.
   count.members = dcount(members, @fm)
   if members = '' then count.members = 0

   if count.members = 0 then
      crt sysmsg(10046, acc.name) ;* Nobody has been granted account %1
      @system.return.code = 0
      return
   end

   crt sysmsg(10047, acc.name) ;* Linux users granted %1:
   for m = 1 to count.members
      crt '   ' : members<m>
   next m

*  THE INSTRUMENT SAYS WHERE ITS ANSWER CAME FROM, which !grp_members' banner
*  asks of its caller: this is the supplementary member list out of /etc/group,
*  and somebody whose PRIMARY group is grp would not be in it.  Nobody on a
*  system SD built is in that position (CREATE.ACCOUNT gives each user a
*  primary group of their own; measured 11 Sep 2026 on all five accounts here),
*  so the line reports a limit rather than a defect - and a reader who has
*  changed a primary group by hand needs to know it.
   crt sysmsg(10911, grp)

   @system.return.code = 0
   return

* ======================================================================
* THE SIGN-OUT LINE IS NOT A PLEASANTRY, and on Linux it says something
* different from the port's, which is why 10043 is reworded rather than copied.
*
* ON WINDOWS THE WHOLE GRANT WAITS FOR THE NEXT LOGON, because group membership
* is fixed in the access token.  ***HERE THE TWO HALVES SEPARATE.***  SD's own
* gates call !is_grp_member, which openpaths /etc and reads "group" on every
* call (IS_GRP_MEMBER, measured by reading it), so LOGIN and LOGTO admit the
* person AT ONCE.  What does not move is the filesystem: the account directory
* is drwxrwsr-x <user> sdu_<name> (measured 11 Sep 2026 on all five accounts
* here) and a Linux session carries the supplementary groups it was given at
* login, so an already-logged-in person is admitted by SD and then meets the
* directory with "other" permission only.
*
* IT APPLIES TO REVOKE JUST AS MUCH, and there it matters more: somebody whose
* grant has been taken away keeps the DIRECTORY access until their next login,
* even though SD has already stopped admitting them.
*
* ***UNWITNESSED, 11 Sep 2026: the split above is read off the modes and the
* code, not measured end to end.***  A witness needs a real grant, so it waits
* for the install cycle - PORT_ADOPTION 14's witness list.  What would falsify
* it: a granted person, still logged in, who can write a record in the account.

say.sign.out:
   crt sysmsg(10043, user) ;* %1 must log out of Linux and back in
   return

* ======================================================================

group.failed:
   @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
   stop sysmsg(10050, grp, rc) ;* Cannot change the members of group %1

end

* END-CODE
